                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "TL:DR 7/10\r\n\r\nAre you looking for a narrative-driven adventure game smothered in spirituality, mythology, the supernatural, and an undying (both figurative and literal) love for NYC? Well then, basically any Wadjet Eye original will suit your fancy. [b][i]This[/i][/b] is the best one.\r\n\r\nIt's a fascinating urban fantasy landscape that matches the vibes of The Blackwell series so closely, it might as well be a spin-off. Its world-building might not withstand the scrutiny of a full-fledged fantasy series, but for a project of this size, it holds together. Occasionally, there's too much exposition. That's an occupational hazard of world-building fiction. Here, much of it is tucked away in optional dialogue, and most players will be eager to learn what drives this universe and its personalities. There were several moments when I braced myself as the dialogue veered into cheese, but all collisions were averted by Wadjet Eye's pitch-perfect voice casting, commitment to the narrative, and earned character arcs. Those arcs unfold through the main plot, but also through incredible interstitial banter between companions and intricately filled environments. I just wish whatever technology inserts voice acting clips could facilitate shorter gaps, as there's inauthenticity in the lagging cadence of conversation.\r\n\r\nThere are an annoying number of ways to play, specifically, paths to take through the game. Self-admittedly annoying to implement, so says Dave Gilbert in the lovely commentary tracks. More importantly, annoying to play. You feel like you're missing out, and you truly are: huge portions of the game's writing are easy to miss without tedious replays. Since each companion has different powers, the puzzles have different solutions depending on who's in your party. This means the puzzles must remain relatively simple. On the other hand, because they're tailored to each character and never too complicated, they're masterfully blended into the story.\r\n\r\nThe aforementioned commentary tracks, as always, offer an insightful glimpse into what it takes to be a brave, semi-famous indie game bard. They reveal the countless invisible design principles, the founts of creativity, and the self-awareness required to cut completed work that doesn't work. It's both inspiring and intimidating: the perfect pump-fake to nudge a wannabe adventure writer to the edge of starting a project, only to remind him that he's not ready. He'll likely never be ready.\r\n",
